  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nForecast remains complex today, with ongoing storms early this \nmorning and the potential evolution of a more significant \nwidespread severe weather/damaging wind threat later this \nmorning and early afternoon. \n\nEarly morning surface analysis depicts a rather diffuse warm \nfrontal zone stretching from north-central IA east-southeast \nacross northern IL and northwest IN, roughly from north of the \nQuad Cities to LOT and VPZ. KLOT VAD wind profile and SPC/RAP \nmesoanalysis indicates a ~25 kt west-southwesterly low level jet\nimpinging on the frontal zone, with associated isentropic \nascent atop the shallow frontal slope resulting in isolated \nthunderstorms from near ARX over to the Lake Michigan shore near\nMichigan City IN at this hour. Low-level jet forcing may \nincrease a bit during the predawn hours as a subtle mid-level \nshort wave tracks across the area. There has been a signal in \nCAM guidance for potential additional development along the \nfront prior to sunrise, and we continue to monitor for that. \nMUCAPE near 3000 J/kg and ~40 kts of effective shear per SPC \nmesoanalysis would support storm organization and an associated \nsevere (primarily wind/hail) threat as evidenced by the \nscattered supercell storms currently over LM/IWX's cwa.\n\nAttention then turns to a developing area of thunderstorms over\nfar southeast South Dakota, which may evolve into a more \nwidespread and potentially significant severe weather threat \nacross our forecast area later this morning and early afternoon.\nCAM guidance has been consistent in this cluster of storms \neventually growing upscale into a forward-propagating MCS across\nnorthwest IA/southwest MN over the next few hours and \neventually tracking east-southeast along the corridor strong \ninstability along the aforementioned frontal zone into northern \nIL toward mid-late morning. The combination of unseasonably \nstrong westerly mid-level flow and resulting deep shear(50+ kts \nat 700 mb), steep mid-level lapse rates (near 8 C/km) MUCAPE \nvalues in excess of 3500 J/kg, the potential for significant \nsevere weather appears rather high. Several CAMs are depicting \npockets surface wind gusts of 80+ mph with this MCS across \nportions of the forecast area. A tornado threat would also \nlikely exist given sufficient low-level shear and the proximity \nof the previously mentioned surface frontal boundary. While the \nexact evolution of this MCS remains uncertain, SPC's Day 1 \nenhanced (level 3 of 5) for out forecast area appears warranted.\nCurrently timing of this threat looks to be from perhaps 9 AM \nin our northwest cwa through around 3 PM in the far east. There \nmay also be some convective development along the front ahead of\nthe MCS, if a strong MCV develops prior to reaching the cwa \nincreases warm-advection ascent ahead of the main cluster as \nindicated by some of the CAMs.\n\nAs if the significant severe/widespread damaging wind threat \nwasn't enough, we'll also need to monitor the location of heavy \nrainfall and flooding potential - particularly for the urbanized\nChicago metro area as well as locations which saw 3-5\"+ of \nrainfall Sunday and evening. Had contemplated issuing a flood \nwatch, though with current predawn convection slow to fill-in \n(so far) and some uncertainty remaining with the evolution of \nthe MCS later this morning, elected to hold off at this time \npending some further clarity on location of higher rainfall \nimpacts. \n\nIn the wake of today's expected MCS, a lull in convective \nactivity is expected from later this afternoon into tonight, \nthough this will likely depend on the size and magnitude of the \nremnant cold pool and how far south the effective frontal zone \nends up along it's southern periphery. The overall pattern some \nuncertainty is how far south an area of surface high pressure \nbuilds across the western Lakes in the Friday/Saturday period. \nThis will have significant impacts on thunderstorm trends/timing\nfor the local area.\n\nRatzer",
